{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"Orxta","id":"343605682580488215","is_bot":0,"username":"robdor."},"author_id":"343605682580488215","channel_id":"715342330978238475","content":"(Here's a Knowledge: Nature lesson for all the suction naysayers, Octopus suction would subside if their muscles relax)\n*When the radial muscles in the sucker contract, the walls of the acetabulum and infundibulum get thinner. The muscle volume has to go somewhere, so the circumference of the cup-shaped acetabulum has to increase, increasing the volume of the acetabular chamber. Since the infundibulum is sealed against a surface, water can't get in; so we have the same quantity of water in a larger chamber, which means the pressure is reduced, generating suction. They can release their grip by relaxing the radial muscles, or contracting the circular muscles, which would reduce the volume of the acetabulum.*","created_at":"2020-07-28T04:03:40.591000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"737520629699313734","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
